{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261024T014655Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "5d20b75fc1534a894734b913", "title": "GLM-5.3 Optimizations, Part 2: PCP, DCP, and Hybrid HiSparse on 16 H200s", "url": "https://vllm.ai/blog/2026-10-23-glm53-part2-pcp-dcp-hisparse", "source": "vLLM Blog", "published": "2026-10-23T00:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.9925, "worthiness_confidence": 0.97, "final_score": 0.9925, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 11683, "delivered": true, "summary": "GLM-5.3 Optimizations, Part 2 presents Prefill Context Parallelism (PCP), Decode Context Parallelism (DCP), and Hybrid HiSparse techniques for serving million-token contexts on 16 H200s. PCP splits prompts by token across GPUs rather than heads, reducing indexer replication and improving compute efficiency from 26 to 191 FLOPs/byte. DCP shards the KV cache by token, enabling 1M-token requests to fit in memory. On AgentX benchmarks, PCP8×DCP8 prefill with Hybrid HiSparse decode achieves 1.7–3.2× better interactivity than data-parallel baselines at equivalent throughput and is the only configuration tested that serves 1M-token contexts.", "jev_ms": 247.8, "jev_input_tokens": 3825, "jev_cost": 0.00016065, "llm_ms": 4690.0, "llm_cost": 0.004623, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261024T014655Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "50cb4eba8a3e4759af97e63d", "title": "vLLM Delivers Day-0 Support for MiniMax-M3.1-Flash", "url": "https://vllm.ai/blog/2026-10-23-minimax-m31-flash", "source": "vLLM Blog", "published": "2026-10-23T00:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.965, "worthiness_confidence": 0.88, "final_score": 0.965, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 17882, "delivered": true, "summary": "vLLM released day-0 support for MiniMax-M3.1-Flash, a sparse attention model with NVFP4 quantization across KV cache and MoE weights, optimized for NVIDIA Blackwell, Rubin, and AMD MI350 GPUs. The model uses block-sparse attention capping queries at 2,048 keys, reducing decode memory bandwidth to 12.5% of dense attention. Key optimizations include CUDA 13.4's QMUL4 instruction for 1.7× faster indexer decode, indexer decode context parallelism for 3.3× speedup at 512K context, and fused QKV+norm+RoPE kernels achieving 1.1–1.6× faster prefill. On agentic workloads, it reaches 724.9 tok/s per user on Rubin and 133.3M tokens per dollar on AMD MI355X.", "jev_ms": 141.4, "jev_input_tokens": 5450, "jev_cost": 0.0002289, "llm_ms": 4714.6, "llm_cost": 0.006816, "errors": []}
